0008 — RELATIONSHIP MODEL
LEGAiSEE COMMANDCENTER KNOWLEDGE SYSTEM

Version: 1.0
Status: ACTIVE GOVERNANCE STANDARD
Scope: /commandcenter/governance/KnowledgeSystem/

1. PURPOSE

This document defines the relationship model used to describe how components inside the LEGAiSEE CommandCenter system interact.

Its purpose is to ensure:

consistent interpretation of dependencies
predictable system reasoning across layers
structured mapping between system components
elimination of ambiguous relational assumptions
stable cognitive model for system analysis
2. CORE PRINCIPLE

All system components MUST be understood as part of a typed relationship graph.

This graph is:

logical, not physical
interpretive, not structural
governance-bound, not dynamic architecture

No physical system changes are implied by this model.

3. RELATIONSHIP TYPES

The system recognizes the following relationship types.

3.1 DEPENDS_ON

Definition:
A component requires another component to function correctly.

Rules:

MUST be explicitly declared in documentation if critical
MUST represent functional necessity, not preference

Example interpretation:

Engine Layer DEPENDS_ON Kernel Layer
3.2 EXTENDS

Definition:
A component adds capability to another component without replacing it.

Rules:

MUST NOT override base behavior
MUST preserve parent system integrity

Example:

predictive_intelligence_engine EXTENDS Engine Layer
3.3 READS_FROM

Definition:
A component consumes data or state from another component.

Rules:

MUST NOT modify source system state
MUST be one-directional unless explicitly defined

Example:

brain_memory_graph READS_FROM Data Layer
3.4 WRITES_TO

Definition:
A component produces or persists data into another component.

Rules:

MUST be validated by governance layer if persistence-critical
MUST follow schema rules of target layer

Example:

ingestion_pipeline WRITES_TO normalized/data/
3.5 ORCHESTRATES

Definition:
A component coordinates execution across multiple systems.

Rules:

MUST maintain deterministic control flow
MUST NOT bypass governance constraints

Example:

brain_orchestrator ORCHESTRATES Engines Layer
3.6 VALIDATES

Definition:
A component enforces rules or correctness checks on another component.

Rules:

MUST be non-destructive
MUST not mutate validated system

Example:

governance Layer VALIDATES Brain Layer outputs
4. LAYER RELATIONSHIP MODEL

The system is organized into five primary logical layers:

4.1 Kernel Layer

Role:
Execution foundation layer.

Relationships:

DOES NOT depend on higher layers
IS depended on by all layers above it
4.2 Brain Layer

Role:
Orchestration and reasoning layer.

Relationships:

DEPENDS_ON Kernel Layer
ORCHESTRATES Engines and Modules
READS_FROM Data Layer
4.3 Engine Layer

Role:
Analytical computation layer.

Relationships:

DEPENDS_ON Kernel Layer
EXTENDS Brain reasoning outputs
READS_FROM Data Layer
4.4 Modules Layer

Role:
Operational interface layer.

Relationships:

DEPENDS_ON Kernel Layer
READS_FROM Brain outputs
WRITES_TO Data Layer (via ingestion or module actions)
4.5 Governance Layer

Role:
System law and enforcement layer.

Relationships:

VALIDATES all layers
MUST NOT be bypassed
HAS authority over execution constraints
4.6 Data Layer

Role:
Persistent storage and memory graph layer.

Relationships:

WRITES_TO by Modules and Engines
READS_FROM by Brain and Engines
DOES NOT execute logic
5. KNOWLEDGE SYSTEM RELATIONSHIP ROLE

The Knowledge System itself:

DOES NOT execute relationships
ONLY describes relationships
ACTS as interpretive mapping layer

It is:

a cognitive overlay
a documentation graph layer
a structural explanation system
6. RELATIONSHIP DIRECTION RULE

All relationships MUST have defined directionality:

A → B (A influences B)
Bidirectional relationships MUST be explicitly declared and justified

Undefined directionality is INVALID.

7. RELATIONSHIP SCOPE RULE

Relationships MUST be classified into one of three scopes:

7.1 Structural Scope

Defines system architecture dependencies

7.2 Operational Scope

Defines runtime interactions

7.3 Governance Scope

Defines rule enforcement and validation paths

Mixing scopes without explicit separation is FORBIDDEN.

8. RELATIONSHIP VALIDATION RULE

Before declaring any relationship:

 Relationship type is explicitly defined
 Direction is unambiguous
 Scope is assigned
 No contradiction with Governance Layer authority
 No architectural mutation is implied

If any condition fails → relationship is INVALID.

9. SYSTEM INTEGRITY PRINCIPLE

The relationship model ensures:

deterministic system interpretation
stable dependency reasoning
consistent cross-layer understanding
long-term system memory coherence

It is a mapping system only, not an execution system.

10. ROLE OF THIS DOCUMENT

This document functions as:

system-wide dependency interpreter
relational logic governor
cross-layer consistency framework
cognitive structure stabilizer for CommandCenter

All future Knowledge System expansions MUST align with this relationship model.

